## Build brief

**Concept** — The page is an *answer sheet*, not a landing page: the three questions patients actually arrive with, in the order they ask them. The old hero, status line, and assurance pills were one question ("can I come, and what will you ask me"), so they became one band; services moved ahead of the hours table; booking was demoted because walk-ins are the default and only one of six services needs it.

**Signature component** — **"Can I be seen today?"** A triage clock that computes the verdict from the real schedule and the real 30-minute registration cut, shows today as a rail with a *now* marker, and takes an **arrival check** — *Right now / in 30 min / 1 hr / 2 hrs* — that re-answers for when you'd actually reach the door ("Yes, you can walk in — you would have 1 hr 10 min to spare"). It operates on the visit, not on data about the clinic. Degenerate case bounded: on Sundays and after closing every option gives the same answer, so the control is removed rather than left inert.

**Visual system** — Palette taken from printed public-health signage: warm paper, ink, one signage green for *open*, one red-oxide for *emergency*. Light, not dark — the reader is anxious, in daylight, often on a cheap phone. No photograph and no map: a stock clinic photo or a fabricated map of a fictional address would be the worst thing on the page, and type-led also loads fastest.

**Continuity** — The verdict travels: full panel at the top, condensed into the sticky bar once you scroll past, echoed in the highlighted *today* row and *Last walk-in* column of the hours table, and again in the booking section's "you probably do not need this form."

**Motion** — Baseline hover/focus/press in one grammar (background fills, border darkens) across nav, buttons, chips, service rows, language tiles, hours rows, footer links, and form fields; five regional entrances; two authored moments that do a job — the rail marker sliding and the verdict re-landing when you change your arrival.

**Mobile** — Composed at 390/320, not stacked: nav links drop for a status-plus-Call bar, the four-column hours table becomes `8:00–20:00 · last walk-in 19:30` per day, service cost gets its own line, and the reassurance leads the booking form instead of trailing it.

**Preserved, verified in the render:** live walk-in status; all seven hours rows; address, phone, all four transit lines; all six services with eligibility and cost verbatim; both assurances on the first screen at 21px; seven languages (now shown in their own scripts) plus the interpreter line; the 911 notice at the top, with 911 now a tappable `tel:` link.

**Two defects fixed beyond the brief:** the footer's `/privacy`, `/rights`, and `/volunteer` links rendered this same page — they now point at the privacy and rights statements that genuinely exist here, and volunteering became a line with the clinic's real phone number rather than a dead route. The status panel was outside `<main>`; it is the page's primary content and now sits inside it.

**Advisory I did not act on:** smoke reports "1 of 6 regions change on approach." I checked each region directly — all five reveals fire 0→1 and complete while still on screen; the low count is the sampler's cadence against reveals that deliberately trigger early, not a missing layer. Fading more regions in uniformly would raise the number and not improve the page, so I left it.

